                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fa33c50b-b5c2-4140-a06e-b666ee5dc5f9",
                                "sense_id": "ff95623e-6d35-3b9e-8b25-95fd9d9f7375",
                                "text": "Users on this Internet chat system are ignorable by right-clicking the user name."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "65ce61f6-cac9-3efd-bc54-77cf3767d327",
                        "entry_id": "a74dc6d6-167f-3cd2-962e-7530edec3b20",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Insignificant or trivial enough to be ignored."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1bd717df-147d-39cd-89e2-f3e979c4eec6",
                        "entry_id": "a74dc6d6-167f-3cd2-962e-7530edec3b20",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Unrelated to the parameters being estimated"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "of missing data"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c85a10c6-0be9-4393-8ff4-c24cb3c9f628",
                                "sense_id": "1bd717df-147d-39cd-89e2-f3e979c4eec6",
                                "text": "Although there is an abundance of statistical literature describing (complicated) methods that can be used to investigate whether or not one is dealing with ignorable or informative missing data in a longitudinal study (see, for instance, Diggle, 1989; Ridout, 1991; Diggle et al., 1994), it is basically quote easy to investigat this matter."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cf95dc82-d4dc-4fa3-a9ec-14fa5eb78ae6",
                                "sense_id": "1bd717df-147d-39cd-89e2-f3e979c4eec6",
                                "text": "Another pertinent distinction in Rubin's missing data classification system is whether the missing data mechanism is ignorable."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9462619c-7edf-4740-bbd4-fcaa1048b2a2",
                                "sense_id": "1bd717df-147d-39cd-89e2-f3e979c4eec6",
                                "text": "If θ and φ are not functionally related to each other (or that the knowledge of one does not provide any information about the other) then the parameters are called “distinct,” and the missing data mechanism is ignorable."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
